eractions [Data: Relationships (12446, 12447, 2327, 2325, 37441)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Processed and raw Chuanwu impact liver function and cardiac tissue\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Both processed (Zhi Chuanwu) and raw (Sheng Chuanwu) forms of Aconiti Radix significantly elevate SGPT levels in animal models, indicating hepatocellular injury. Processed Chuanwu is specifically linked to SGPT elevation and myocardial cell vacuolar degeneration, a histopathological sign of cardiotoxicity. Additionally, processed Chuanwu exhibits anti-inflammatory effects, potentially mediated through gut microbiota metabolism leading to increased benzoylhypaconitine. These dual effects—therapeutic anti-inflammatory action alongside organ toxicity—highlight the narrow therapeutic window of Chuanwu preparations. Notably, co-administration with Baizhi (Bletillae Rhizoma) mitigates SGPT elevation, suggesting a protective herb-herb interaction [Data: Relationships (1038, 3292, 9398, 9400, 9401)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Huaihua has multiple contraindications in traditional Chinese medicine patterns\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Huaihua (Sophorae Flos) is contraindicated in several TCM-defined patient populations, including those with spleen-stomach deficiency-cold, internal cold from raw food consumption (neishang shengleng), kidney yang deficiency, early-stage heat dysentery, and blood stasis. These contraindications reflect traditional principles cautioning against using cold-natured herbs in patients with underlying deficiency or cold syndromes. Additionally, Huaihua lowers blood pressure, making it unsuitable for hypotensive individuals. It also interacts with metal-containing Western medications like aluminum hydroxide formulations, forming complexes that reduce absorption. This illustrates a clinically relevant herb-drug interaction that could compromise efficacy [Data: Relationships (6529, 6522, 6528, 6525, 6526, 